{
 "id": "0-2-1",
 "title": "Currents, Tides and Ocean Resources",
 "emoji": "🌐",
 "cards": [
  {
   "tag": "Why this matters",
   "h": "Moving water shapes trade and climate",
   "b": "<p>Currents decide where deserts, fog and fisheries occur. Tides decide when ships can enter ports. The sea also supplies fish, oil, gas and minerals. These are core topics for GS I and for the <b>blue economy</b> theme in current affairs.</p>"
  },
  {
   "tag": "Core idea",
   "h": "What drives currents",
   "b": "<svg class=\"d\" viewBox=\"0 0 320 190\"><defs><marker id=\"gyA\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z\" fill=\"#5d6474\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"40\" y=\"20\" width=\"240\" height=\"140\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dceaec\" stroke=\"#2f6f7a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M70 120 L70 60 L250 60\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#a3362a\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#gyA)\"/><path d=\"M250 60 L250 120 L70 120\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2f6f7a\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#gyA)\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"52\" fill=\"#a3362a\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Warm current (e.g. Gulf Stream)</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"138\" fill=\"#2f6f7a\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Cold current (e.g. Canary)</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"92\" fill=\"#5d6474\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Clockwise gyre, north hemisphere</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"106\" fill=\"#5d6474\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Anticlockwise in the south</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#5d6474\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Coriolis force and trade winds create gyres.</text></svg><p><b>Currents</b> are large streams of water. Drivers: prevailing <b>winds</b>, <b>Coriolis force</b>, differences in <b>temperature and salinity</b> (density), gravity and coastlines. Currents flow clockwise in the northern gyres and anticlockwise in the southern.</p>"
  },
  {
   "tag": "Core idea",
   "h": "Warm and cold currents",
   "b": "<svg class=\"d\" viewBox=\"0 0 320 158\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"6\" width=\"90\" height=\"26\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f1e4c6\" stroke=\"#a8792a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"55.0\" y=\"23\" fill=\"#7d5a1c\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Warm</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"6\" width=\"100\" height=\"26\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f1e4c6\" stroke=\"#a8792a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"150.0\" y=\"23\" fill=\"#7d5a1c\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Cold</text><rect x=\"200\" y=\"6\" width=\"110\" height=\"26\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f1e4c6\" stroke=\"#a8792a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"255.0\" y=\"23\" fill=\"#7d5a1c\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Effect</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"32\" width=\"90\" height=\"30\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"55.0\" y=\"51\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Gulf Stream</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"32\" width=\"100\" height=\"30\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"150.0\" y=\"51\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Labrador</text><rect x=\"200\" y=\"32\" width=\"110\" height=\"30\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"255.0\" y=\"51\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Fog at Grand Banks</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"62\" width=\"90\" height=\"30\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"55.0\" y=\"81\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Kuroshio</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"62\" width=\"100\" height=\"30\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"150.0\" y=\"81\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Oyashio</text><rect x=\"200\" y=\"62\" width=\"110\" height=\"30\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"255.0\" y=\"81\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Fisheries, Japan</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"92\" width=\"90\" height=\"30\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"55.0\" y=\"111\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Brazil</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"92\" width=\"100\" height=\"30\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"150.0\" y=\"111\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Benguela</text><rect x=\"200\" y=\"92\" width=\"110\" height=\"30\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"255.0\" y=\"111\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Namib desert</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"122\" width=\"90\" height=\"30\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"55.0\" y=\"141\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Agulhas</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"122\" width=\"100\" height=\"30\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"150.0\" y=\"141\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Humboldt (Peru)</text><rect x=\"200\" y=\"122\" width=\"110\" height=\"30\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"255.0\" y=\"141\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Atacama desert</text></svg><p>Warm currents flow from low to high latitudes. Cold currents flow towards the equator, usually along west coasts. Where they meet, as off Newfoundland, <b>fog</b> forms and plankton thrives, making rich fisheries (Grand Banks, Japan, North Sea).</p>"
  },
  {
   "tag": "Core idea",
   "h": "Effects of currents on climate",
   "b": "<p>The <b>North Atlantic Drift</b> keeps north-west European ports like Bergen ice-free. Cold currents along west coasts cut rainfall and form <b>coastal deserts</b>: Atacama and Namib. The Humboldt current is linked to El Nino collapses in Peru's anchovy fishery.</p>"
  },
  {
   "tag": "Core idea",
   "h": "Indian Ocean: currents that reverse",
   "b": "<p>Unlike the Atlantic and Pacific, the northern Indian Ocean has <b>seasonally reversing</b> currents, driven by the monsoon. In summer, the <b>South-West Monsoon Drift</b> flows eastward. In winter, the <b>North-East Monsoon Drift</b> flows westward. South of 10 S the circulation is a steady anticlockwise gyre.</p><p>Hence ancient sailors used the monsoon to cross.</p>"
  },
  {
   "tag": "Core idea",
   "h": "Tides",
   "b": "<svg class=\"d\" viewBox=\"0 0 320 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"8\" width=\"145\" height=\"60\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f1e4c6\" stroke=\"#a8792a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"82\" y=\"28\" fill=\"#7d5a1c\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Spring tide</text><text x=\"82\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#5d6474\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sun, Earth, Moon aligned</text><text x=\"82\" y=\"58\" fill=\"#5d6474\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">New and full moon</text><rect x=\"165\" y=\"8\" width=\"145\" height=\"60\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dceaec\" stroke=\"#2f6f7a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"237\" y=\"28\" fill=\"#2f6f7a\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Neap tide</text><text x=\"237\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#5d6474\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sun and Moon at 90 degrees</text><text x=\"237\" y=\"58\" fill=\"#5d6474\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Quarter moons</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"78\" width=\"300\" height=\"64\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f3f1ec\" stroke=\"#c9c2b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"98\" fill=\"#1d2433\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Tides: 2 highs and 2 lows each day (about 12 h 26 min apart)</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"116\" fill=\"#5d6474\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Moon's pull is about twice the Sun's. Spring tides are highest.</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"130\" fill=\"#5d6474\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Perigee (Moon near) raises range; apogee lowers it.</text></svg><p>Tides come from the Moon's and Sun's gravity and Earth's spin. Narrow, funnel-shaped bays raise the range: the Bay of Fundy (about 16 m) is the highest. Tide energy and port design use these data.</p>"
  },
  {
   "tag": "Core idea",
   "h": "Ocean resources",
   "b": "<p><b>Living:</b> fish, seaweed. <b>Mineral:</b> salt, magnesium, nodules, sand. <b>Energy:</b> oil and gas (Mumbai High), tidal and wave power, <b>gas hydrates</b>. The Law of the Sea (UNCLOS, 1982) gives a 12 nautical mile territorial sea, a 200 nautical mile <b>EEZ</b>, and rights to the extended continental shelf.</p>"
  },
  {
   "tag": "Debate",
   "h": "Blue economy: growth or damage?",
   "b": "<p>The ocean may be a new growth engine, but overfishing, plastic and bottom trawling threaten it. The Gulf Stream-linked <b>AMOC</b> may be slowing due to meltwater, which could shift weather in Europe and monsoon patterns. Science is still unsure about timing.</p>"
  },
  {
   "tag": "Indian angle",
   "h": "India's tides and blue economy",
   "b": "<p>India has large tides in the Gulf of Khambhat and the Gulf of Kutch (up to about 11 m at Kandla); both are tidal power sites. The Hooghly sees a tidal bore.</p><p>India is among the top fish producers, with about 8% of the global total (as of recent data). <b>Sagarmala</b> develops ports; the <b>Deep Ocean Mission</b> (2021) and a Blue Economy policy frame the push. Mumbai High remains the largest offshore oil field.</p>"
  },
  {
   "tag": "Exam lens",
   "cls": "exam",
   "h": "How to write this",
   "b": "<div class=\"box\"><h4>Likely questions</h4><p>\"Describe the ocean currents of the Indian Ocean and how they differ from those of the Atlantic.\" (10 marks)</p><p>\"How do ocean currents influence the climate of coastal areas?\" (10 marks)</p></div><p><b>Structure:</b> causes; examples in a table; effects (rain, fog, fisheries); Indian Ocean reversal; end with policy.</p><p><b>Prelims tip:</b> cold currents on west coasts of continents at 15-30 degrees; spring tides occur at new and full moon; EEZ is 200 nm.</p>"
  }
 ],
